# Template for agent/.env
#
# THIS file (.env.example) is committed to git and holds fake values only. The
# real file - agent/.env - is gitignored twice over, by the root .gitignore
# and again by agent/.gitignore, and never leaves your machine.
#
# To set up:
#   Windows PowerShell:  Copy-Item .env.example .env
# then fill in the real key below.
#
# Read by agent/config.py, which loads this file at import time and validates
# it. A missing required setting stops the process at startup with a message
# naming the field - it does not surface later as a confusing 401.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GEMINI_API_KEY - required. An AI Studio key for the Gemini Developer API.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Get one free at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey.
#
# This is the free tier discussed in docs/AGENT-PLAN.md. Two consequences
# worth knowing before you wonder why something is failing:
#
#   - Pro models are not available on it. Flash-class only, which is one of
#     the reasons Flash was chosen rather than a constraint discovered late.
#   - The limits are real and low, and one conversational turn is SEVERAL
#     model calls, not one - each tool call round-trips through the model
#     again. A single user question can exhaust a per-minute budget by itself.
#     Gate 16 records this account's actual limits from
#     https://aistudio.google.com/rate-limit.

GEMINI_API_KEY=your-ai-studio-key-here

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE_URL - required. Same Supabase Postgres instance backend/ uses,
# a different schema (`agent`, not `public`). Get the connection string the
# same way backend/.env.example describes: Supabase dashboard -> Connect ->
# Session pooler tab, then swap the scheme and append sslmode as shown below.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://postgres.your-project-ref:YOUR-PASSWORD@aws-0-eu-west-3.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?sslmode=require

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything below is optional - these have defaults in config.py.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which model to call. Leave it commented out and you get `gemma-31b`.
#
# Three short names are recognised, resolved to real model IDs by config.py's
# MODELS table. Pick ONE line; leaving all three commented is the same as the
# first:
#
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemma-31b     # -> gemma-4-31b-it       (default)
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemma-26b     # -> gemma-4-26b-a4b-it   (faster/cheaper, MoE)
# GEMINI_MODEL=flash-lite    # -> gemini-3.5-flash-lite (the Gemini comparison)
#
# A full model ID also works, so a model Google ships next month needs no code
# change - that is the point of this being a setting. A short name that is not in
# the list is rejected at startup instead, since that can only be a typo.
#
# **Which to use.** Gemma is the development default on quota grounds: the
# free-tier dashboard showed Flash-Lite at 500 requests/day against Gemma's
# ~10-20k, and one conversational turn is several model calls. Switch to
# flash-lite when the question is whether GEMMA is the problem - it is a
# different family with a different function-calling implementation, so it is a
# real second opinion rather than a bigger version of the same thing.
#
# All three support function calling; Gemma's was verified against
# https://ai.google.dev/gemma/docs/core/gemma_on_gemini_api on 2026-08-11,
# because a model that cannot call tools would be useless to this project. The
# known caveat is that Gemma reaches for tools less readily than Gemini unless
# told to, which is why scripts/ask.py prints the tool calls it made.
#
# Note the default is NOT a `-latest` alias. Those point at the newest release,
# which is also the one most likely to return 503 under load - it did exactly
# that on the first call at Gate 15a.

# Which provider class in model_provider.py builds the Model. Defaults to
# "google", the only registered provider today - both Gemini and Gemma go
# through it, since both are the same Google API and differ only in
# GEMINI_MODEL's value. Change this only when adding a genuinely different
# provider SDK (Anthropic, OpenAI); switching between Gemini and Gemma is a
# GEMINI_MODEL change, not this one.
# MODEL_PROVIDER=google

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MCP_BASE_URL - where the backend's MCP server is listening. Used from Gate
# 15c onward. Defaults to http://127.0.0.1:8001/mcp.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# DO NOT point this at a non-loopback address without reading
# docs/AGENT-PLAN.md's "stop condition" section first.
#
# **Gate 25 changed this, and the rule below still stands.** The agent and the
# MCP server used to run with no authentication at all; now the agent demands a
# bearer token and the MCP server verifies one against ThunderID's published
# keys. What has NOT changed: nothing rate-limits an anonymous caller,
# ThunderID's certificate is self-signed, and AUTH_ENABLED is one boolean away
# from turning all of it off silently. Those are Gate 26's list.
#
# So the socket stays bound to 127.0.0.1, and that binding is still what makes
# this safe today. Changing it - a LAN address,
# ngrok, a Cloudflare tunnel, a Docker port publish, a cloud deploy - exposes a
# tool surface that can mutate real inventory, to anyone, with no log entry
# distinguishing them from you.
#
# It is written out as a flat list of forbidden actions rather than a
# principle because the dangerous version of this mistake is not a decision
# someone weighs. It is "I'll just tunnel it so I can demo from my phone" - a
# thirty-second convenience. A principle can be reasoned around at speed; a
# list cannot.
#
# Any of those changes triggers the auth gate BEFORE further agent work.

# MCP_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8001/mcp


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ThunderID - the login server. Gate 25.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The agent is an OAuth *client*: unlike the backend, it holds a secret and
# talks to ThunderID directly. What it does with it is one thing - trade the
# signed-in person's token for a NARROWER one, valid only at the MCP server.
# ThunderID cannot hand back more authority than the token it was given, which
# is how "the agent may only do what you may do" is enforced somewhere our own
# bugs cannot reach.

# The agent's own credentials - the `AIsle Agent` application, registered
# 2026-08-25. The secret is shown ONCE in the console; if it is lost, a new one
# must be generated (which invalidates the old).
#
# ⚠️ Registered as `client_secret_basic`, so they travel in an HTTP Basic
# header, not in the form body. `client_secret_post` is answered with
# `unauthorized_client`, which reads like a permissions problem and is not one.
#
# Optional: without them the agent still starts, and fails only when it
# actually tries to exchange a token. That is deliberate - a machine running
# with AUTH_ENABLED=false was never going to need them.
# THUNDERID_CLIENT_ID=
# THUNDERID_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Where token exchange happens. Defaults to the local container.
# THUNDERID_TOKEN_URL=https://localhost:8090/oauth2/token

# Which service the exchanged token is minted for: the `Agentic ERP MCP`
# resource server, NOT the HTTP API.
#
# ⚠️ This becomes the token's `aud` because it is sent as RFC 8707 `resource`.
# ThunderID also accepts an `audience` parameter and then ignores it - verified
# twice against a live server. Nothing here should ever send `audience`.
# THUNDERID_MCP_AUDIENCE=https://mcp.agentic-erp.local

# The ceiling on what the agent may ask for on your behalf, space-delimited,
# in the same vocabulary services/ uses.
#
# ⚠️ A ceiling, not a grant. ThunderID returns the intersection of this, your
# own permissions and the agent's role - and it NARROWS SILENTLY: asking for
# too much returns 200 OK with less, and asking for a permission that does not
# exist returns 200 OK with no scope claim at all. agent/auth.py therefore
# checks what came back rather than trusting the status code.
# ⚠️ NOTE THE ASYMMETRY WITH THE WEB APP. The browser asks for `draft.decide`
# and the agent does not, and that is the whole point of the approval queue:
# the agent proposes, a human decides. Copying the web app's list here would
# quietly remove one of the three layers enforcing that. See
# docs/FEATURES-PLAN.md, decision 1.
# THUNDERID_SCOPES=product.read product.create product.update stock.adjust draft.read draft.create lot.read purchasing.read

# ⚠️ LOCAL ONLY. ThunderID's development certificate is self-signed, so an
# ordinary HTTPS call to it fails certificate validation. The better fix is to
# trust that one certificate (this setting, which still refuses an attacker's);
# the blunt one is to stop checking (the next setting). The first is tried
# first and the second is only a fallback.
#
# Defaults to deploy/thunderid-server.cert, which the local Compose run writes
# out. That file is gitignored because every machine generates its own, so it
# is often absent - and its absence is not an error.
# THUNDERID_CA_CERT=

# ⚠️ LOCAL ONLY, and worse than the setting above: no certificate checking at
# all. Anyone able to intercept the connection to ThunderID can hand the agent
# a token of their own devising. Gate 26 removes the need for both by giving
# ThunderID a real certificate.
# THUNDERID_VERIFY_TLS=false

# The escape hatch, matching backend/.env.example's. False means the agent
# accepts any caller as an all-powerful actor and sends no token onward - the
# pre-gate-25 behaviour, for the test suite and for local work unrelated to
# auth. Defaults to true so that FORGETTING to configure auth fails closed.
# NEVER set it false anywhere reachable by anything but this machine.
# AUTH_ENABLED=false
